A workshop exercise uses the following code:
const v = 1100;
console.log(`n=${v}`.length);
What length is printed?

Study the program and name its final output:
const text = 'id=1101';
console.log(/\d+/.test(text));
What boolean is printed?

A chalkboard example uses the program below:
const arr = [1102, 2, 3];
console.log(arr.includes(1102));
On this input, explain what this code prints and why that value follows from the language rules.

A test harness runs these statements in order:
const arr = [1103, 8, 5];
arr.push(38);
console.log(arr.length);
From this trace, what number is printed by the final log?

A junior developer pastes this snippet into the console:
const arr = [1104, 75, 38, 96];
const part = arr.slice(1, 3);
